//
// A value that does NOT FIT a non-nullable narrow slot takes the type's DEFAULT — zero where
// the declared range holds it, the bound nearest zero otherwise — and it takes the SAME one
// in a local, a field, an element, a keyed collection's key, a by-value parameter and a
// return (`DESIGN_DECISIONS.md` C127, `formal/types.md` `(N-Reserve)`, `formal/operational.md`
// `(E-Uncomp-NN)`, `formal/layout.md` `(L-Narrow-Spare)`).  A narrow type without `?` has no
// null, so no slot of one ever reads absent.
//
// The types here are the ones that make that a question rather than a restatement: a signed
// narrow alias whose declared range leaves codes unused inside its storage width.
// `limit(-100, 100) size(1)` is 201 values in 256; `limit(-1000, 1000) size(2)` is 2001 in
// 65536.  A type whose range FILLS its width — `u8`, `i16` — has no spare code to argue about,
// and `fullwidth` below is the control that pins it answering the same way.
//
// Two rounds of defects met here, and the cells are laid out so each is still addressable.
//
// The DISAGREEMENT (loft#1615) — the local was right and the store places were not, on both
// backends: the one-byte write cast its value `as i32` before the store saw it, and the
// two-byte read decoded all 65536 codes as values, so one overflow landed as `0` and the
// other read back `64535`, 63535 past the type's own maximum.  `data::NarrowSlot::of_slot`
// is the one home for which ops a store place takes, asked by `Parser::get_val` (the read)
// and `Parser::set_field_check` (the write) alike, so the pair cannot drift again.
//
// The VALUE (C127) — every one of those slots then agreed on NULL, because the type had a
// code to spare.  Whether `hi - lo + 1` falls short of `2^(8*size)` is arithmetic the author
// did not do: `limit(-128, 127) size(1)` answered `0` and `limit(-100, 100) size(1)` answered
// null, two declarations one value apart, and widening a range for an unrelated reason
// flipped an overflow from detectable to silent.  `parser::expressions::uncomputable_default`
// is the one home, and it now answers the type's default for every non-nullable spec.
//
// Every cell states its expected value by hand.  The cells that can FAIL are the ones whose
// value leaves the range; the controls are what stop a cure reaching too far:
//
//   * `ta`/`tb` — the untouched twin of each overflowed field, at its type's MAX and MIN.
//     Those are the two codes nearest the one an overflow used to take, so a fix one code
//     too wide erases them.
//   * `v1`/`w1` — an element never written past its range, and deliberately NOT the default:
//     `33` and `44`, because a control holding `0` is indistinguishable from the cell beside
//     it and could not fail.
//   * `nullables` — the `τ?` spelling of both types.  This is the half C127 did NOT move: a
//     nullable narrow HAS a null, C85 writes its sentinel, and a fix that confused the two
//     would take it away.  `na_over`/`nb_over` overflow one, so the two answers sit side by
//     side in one file: `τ` gives the default, `τ?` gives null.
//   * `fullwidth` — `u8` and `i16` fill their width and never had a spare code, so they
//     answered the default before C127 and must still.
//   * `keyed` — a narrow field as a hash KEY and as a sorted collection's key, neither
//     overflowed: a wrong decode there loses the record, not just the number.
//
// The other cells are the reach: the overflow through `+=`, `-=` and `*=`, a field read
// through a by-value struct PARAMETER and out of a CALL's return, the read as a condition,
// as a `??` subject, and repeated in a loop.
//
// `matrix_axes.py` reports this file missing `index` and `spatial` containers and the
// float/boolean/enum element types: those are axes of the CONTAINER idioms, and this is a
// question about one scalar slot's encoding, which every container reaches through the same
// two parser sites the fix routes through.  Its `A2` row (parameter, callee-return) is
// covered next door, in `1634-a-narrow-types-default-is-the-same-at-every-frame-boundary`.
//
// Its `A3 … MISSING tuple-element` was NOT a false gap and is why running the tool on a
// finished guard is worth the minute: a TUPLE MEMBER is a slot kind `(N-Reserve)` does not
// name, and a plain assignment into one is unbounded — `t: (u8, u8) = (250, 7); t.0 = 300`
// stores 300, where the local, the field and the element this file already covers all refuse
// it (loft#1640, `formal/types.md` D-types-2).  It is NOT added here: this file is about the
// value an unfitting write TAKES, and there the write is not refused at all, which is a
// different defect with a different cure.  The cell belongs with that fix.
//
// Two lints now fire on `contexts()`, and they are pinned rather than edited away: with C127
// the compiler KNOWS a non-nullable narrow field cannot read null, so `s.a == null` is a
// constant and `s.a ?? -7` never takes its default.  Both went silent for a spare-code type
// before C127 while firing for `limit(-128, 127) size(1)` next door, so their presence here
// is the compile-time half of the same answer the assertions check at run time.
//
// @EXPECT_WARNING: Warning[redundant-null-check]
// @EXPECT_WARNING: Warning[redundant-coalesce]
//
// @falsified-at: 53f2c6bd3 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 1 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
// HOLDS: leak, panic and free-refusal are clean on both trees and both backends; no durable
//   patch, because 53f2c6bd3 is 4083 source lines from this tree and a diff against it would
//   reintroduce everything fixed in between rather than this defect.
//
//   What the control actually answers, measured 2026-09-23 against the cached 53f2c6bd3
//   build (both backends agreeing):
//
//     locals  x=null  y=null          the local answered C85's null
//     fields  a=0     b=64535         the field answered the stored CODE as a value
//
//   Against C127 every one of those is wrong — the local for answering null at all, the
//   two-byte field for reporting 64535 (63535 past the type's own maximum), and the
//   one-byte field for reaching `0` by TRUNCATION rather than by default.  That last cell
//   is the one worth naming: `a=0` equals what C127 requires, so it is the one cell in this
//   file that would pass at the control and pass for the wrong reason.  A receipt built on
//   it alone would be measuring nothing.
//
// CHANNEL: the assertions.

type S8  = integer limit(-100, 100) size(1);
type S16 = integer limit(-1000, 1000) size(2);

struct F  { a: S8, b: S16 }
struct N  { a: S8?, b: S16? }
struct W  { u: u8, i: i16 }
struct T  { w: integer, i: i32, u: u32 }
struct K  { id: S8, b: S16 }

fn show(label: text, v: integer) -> text { "{label}={v}/{v == null}" }

// The answer every other slot must match: a LOCAL of each type, overflowed, and the two edge
// values that must stay values.
fn locals() -> text {
  x: S8 = 100; x += 50;
  y: S16 = -1000; y -= 1;
  p: S8 = 100;
  q: S16 = -1000;
  "{show("x", x)} {show("y", y)} {show("p", p)} {show("q", q)}"
}

fn fields() -> text {
  s = F { a: 100, b: -1000 };
  t = F { a: 100, b: -1000 };
  s.a += 50; s.b -= 1;
  "{show("a", s.a)} {show("b", s.b)} {show("ta", t.a)} {show("tb", t.b)}"
}

// The other spellings of a write that leaves the range.  A checked cast is NOT one: it is
// discharged at the cast and the `??` default lands as a value.
fn field_forms() -> text {
  s = F { a: 0, b: 0 };
  n: integer = 300;
  s.a = n as S8 ?? -100;
  u = "{show("cast", s.a)}";
  s.a = 100; s.a *= 2;
  s.b = -1000; s.b *= 2;
  "{u} {show("mul", s.a)} {show("mul16", s.b)}"
}

// `v1` and `w1` are 33 and 44 rather than 0 on purpose: a control holding the same value as
// the cell it guards cannot fail.
fn elements() -> text {
  v: vector<S8> = [100, 33];
  w: vector<S16> = [-1000, 44];
  v[0] += 50; w[0] -= 1;
  "{show("v0", v[0] ?? 7)} {show("v1", v[1] ?? 7)} {show("w0", w[0] ?? 7)} {show("w1", w[1] ?? 7)}"
}

// The half C127 did not move: a nullable narrow HAS a null, and an overflow writes it.
fn nullables() -> text {
  s = N { a: null, b: null };
  u = "{s.a == null} {s.b == null}";
  s.a = 100; s.b = -1000;
  v = "{show("na", s.a ?? 7)} {show("nb", s.b ?? 7)}";
  o = N { a: 100, b: -1000 };
  o.a += 50; o.b -= 1;
  "{u} {v} na_over={o.a == null} nb_over={o.b == null}"
}

fn fullwidth() -> text {
  s = W { u: 255, i: -32768 };
  t = W { u: 255, i: -32768 };
  t.u += 1; t.i -= 1;
  "{show("u", s.u)} {show("i", s.i)} {show("uo", t.u)} {show("io", t.i)}"
}

fn keyed() -> text {
  h: hash<K[id]> = [];
  h += [K { id: 100, b: -1000 }];
  e = h[100];
  o: text = "miss";
  if e != null { o = "{show("hid", e.id)} {show("hb", e.b)}"; }
  srt: sorted<K[id]> = [];
  srt += [K { id: -100, b: 0 }];
  f = srt[-100];
  o2: text = "miss";
  if f != null { o2 = show("sid", f.id); }
  "{o} {o2}"
}

// C127's LINE, in one function: a DECLARED range gives the default, the plain `integer` and
// `i32` TEMPLATES keep C85's sentinel — and each answers the same in a local, a field and an
// element.  `u32` sits on the declared side (its range is `0..=u32::MAX - 1`, matching neither
// template), which is what makes it the cell that cannot be passed by a rule about widths.
//
// This row pins a DECISION and not a mechanism, which is why it is worth its length.  The
// `i32` exemption is PRAGMATIC (C127 § Decision, owner 2026-09-23): C127's rule reaches `i32`
// perfectly well, and it is exempted because reserving `i32::MIN` costs one value in 2^32 and
// buys the only narrow type whose overflow is detectable.  So the `i32` column here is
// REVISITABLE in a way the others are not — `u32` is the proof that the split is not a
// principle, since it reserves a code at the top of its range and takes the default anyway.
// If that cost assessment ever changes, this is the row that moves, and having all three
// types across all three slot kinds in one assertion is what makes the full extent of the
// move visible in one failure instead of six.
//
// The axis this exists for is `not_null`.  Every non-nullable struct FIELD's spec carries that
// flag, and it is a claim about the slot rather than about the bytes: a field declared `i: i32`
// still has the range `[i32::MIN + 1, MAX]`, so `i32::MIN` is still spare in it.  Asking the
// stored default through a predicate that reads `not_null` first made the `i32` FIELD answer
// `0` while its local and its element answered null — loft#1296's disagreement reopened from
// the other side, and caught by `1030-compound-range-both-spellings` rather than by this file,
// which is why the row is here now.
fn templates() -> text {
  lw: integer = 9223372036854775807; lw += 1;
  li: i32 = 2147483647;              li += 10;
  lu: u32 = 4294967290;              lu += 10;
  t = T { w: 9223372036854775807, i: 2147483647, u: 4294967290 };
  t.w += 1; t.i += 10; t.u += 10;
  vw: vector<integer> = [9223372036854775807]; vw[0] += 1;
  vi: vector<i32> = [2147483647];              vi[0] += 10;
  vu: vector<u32> = [4294967290];              vu[0] += 10;
  "local {lw == null} {li == null} {lu} field {t.w == null} {t.i == null} {t.u} "
  + "elem {vw[0] == null} {vi[0] == null} {vu[0] ?? -1}"
}

fn take(v: F) -> text { "{show("pa", v.a)} {show("pb", v.b)}" }
fn make() -> F { g = F { a: 100, b: -1000 }; g.a += 50; g.b -= 1; g }
fn provenance() -> text { "{take(make())} {show("ra", make().a)}" }

// The loop accumulates the VALUE and not the nullness: with C127 every pass reads non-null,
// so a nullness trace would be three identical characters and could not fail.
fn contexts() -> text {
  s = F { a: 100, b: -1000 };
  s.a += 50; s.b -= 1;
  c = if s.a == null { "cond-null" } else { "cond-value" };
  d = s.a ?? -7;
  acc = "";
  t = F { a: 0, b: 0 };
  for i in 0..3 { t.a = 100; t.a += 50 * i; acc += "{t.a},"; }
  "{c} coal={d} loop={acc}"
}

fn main() {
  assert(locals() == "x=0/false y=0/false p=100/false q=-1000/false",
         "the local's answer, which every other slot must match: {locals()}");
  assert(fields() == "a=0/false b=0/false ta=100/false tb=-1000/false",
         "a narrow field after an overflow: {fields()}");
  assert(field_forms() == "cast=-100/false mul=0/false mul16=0/false",
         "the other write spellings: {field_forms()}");
  assert(elements() == "v0=0/false v1=33/false w0=0/false w1=44/false",
         "a narrow element after an overflow: {elements()}");
  assert(nullables() == "true true na=100/false nb=-1000/false na_over=true nb_over=true",
         "the nullable spelling keeps its null: {nullables()}");
  assert(fullwidth() == "u=255/false i=-32768/false uo=0/false io=0/false",
         "a type that fills its width answers the same default: {fullwidth()}");
  assert(templates() == "local true true 0 field true true 0 elem true true 0",
         "templates keep C85's sentinel, `u32` takes the default: {templates()}");
  assert(keyed() == "hid=100/false hb=-1000/false sid=-100/false",
         "a narrow key and element field: {keyed()}");
  assert(provenance() == "pa=0/false pb=0/false ra=0/false",
         "through a by-value parameter and a call's return: {provenance()}");
  assert(contexts() == "cond-value coal=0 loop=100,0,0,",
         "as a condition, a coalesce subject and in a loop: {contexts()}");
  println("every narrow slot answers the type's default for an unfitting value");
}
